+### Creating a JSON Patch — `createPatch(from, to)`
+
+Compares two versions of a JSON document and returns the JSON Patch that turns the first into the
+second. It is the other direction of `applyPatch`: what a service calls when it holds both versions
+and only the change should go over the wire.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch, createPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const stored = { name: 'disk', labels: { tier: 'cold', region: 'eu' }, sizes: [1, 2, 3] };
+const wanted = { name: 'disk', labels: { tier: 'hot' }, sizes: [1, 2, 3, 4] };
+
+const patch = createPatch(stored, wanted);
+// [
+//   { op: 'replace', path: '/labels/tier', value: 'hot' },
+//   { op: 'remove', path: '/labels/region' },
+//   { op: 'add', path: '/sizes/3', value: 4 },
+// ]
+
+applyPatch(stored, patch); // the `wanted` document
+```
+
+The patch says what differs and nothing else: `name` is not in it, nor are the three elements `sizes`
+keeps. Two documents that do not differ give an empty patch, and one changed member of a document of
+any size is one operation.
+
+| difference                                | what the patch says                                                                       |
+| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| a member added, removed or changed        | one `add`, `remove` or `replace` at that member — a change *inside* it one level deeper    |
+| an array element inserted or dropped      | one `add` or `remove` at its index; the elements around it are not sent                    |
+| an array element changed                  | the difference inside that element, at its index                                           |
+| values of different kinds                 | one `replace` of the whole value — `path: ""` when it is the document itself               |
+
+Points worth knowing:
+
+- the patch is made of `add`, `remove` and `replace`. `test` states a precondition rather than a
+  change, so a caller who wants the update refused when the document has moved under them prepends
+  their own `test`; `move` and `copy` say nothing a `remove` and an `add` do not already say, and
+  would cost a search through the document for a value that may be anywhere in it;
+- an array is compared as the sequence it is, by the fewest element edits, so inserting one element
+  at the front of a thousand is one `add` and not a thousand `replace`s. The operations on an array
+  are written against the array as the operations before them leave it, which is how RFC 6902 applies
+  them: removing two elements in a row can name the same index twice;
+- the same two documents always give the same patch, in the same order: `from`'s members in its
+  order, then the members only `to` has, in its order;
+- when nearly every part of a value changed, the patch describes each part rather than replacing the
+  value whole. That is more operations than a single `replace` of the parent would be, and it is the
+  deliberate trade: every operation in a patch corresponds to something that really changed, and
+  which patch a pair of documents gives never depends on a size threshold;
+- neither document is changed, and the patch shares no object or array with either — the values it
+  carries are copies taken from `to`, so all three can be changed afterwards independently;
+- comparing cannot fail, so `createPatch` throws nothing: any two JSON values differ in a way a patch
+  can state.
+
