+---
+id: dl-004-shape-and-semantics-of-createpatch-what-a-diff-describes-how-arrays-are-aligned-which-operations-it-never-produces
+type: decision-log
+title: "Shape and semantics of createPatch: what a diff describes, how arrays are aligned, which operations it never produces"
+status: approved
+---
+
+# Shape and semantics of `createPatch`: what a diff describes, how arrays are aligned, which operations it never produces
+
+## Context
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` is the fourth building block and the first that *writes* a format instead of
+reading one: the three before it take a document and something to do to it, while this one takes two
+documents and produces the JSON Patch that turns the first into the second. The clients that already
+send `applyPatch` patches now hold both versions of a document and want to send the change alone.
+
+RFC 6902 specifies what a patch *means*, not which patch two documents deserve. Any patch whose
+application turns `from` into `to` is correct, including the single `replace` of the whole document
+that `createPatch` could always return — and the request rules that one out: a patch must describe
+what changed and nothing more. So almost everything decided here is about *which* of the correct
+patches to produce, which is a choice the RFC leaves open and therefore one that has to be written
+down: callers will compare patches against expected ones, and a patch that changes shape between
+versions of this library would break them.
+
+Two of the earlier decisions carry over without argument: documents are `JsonValue` (`dl-001`), and
+the result shares nothing with its inputs (`dl-002`, `dl-003`). One does not: `dl-002` and `dl-003`
+are largely about failure, and comparing two documents has no failure mode at all — any two JSON
+values differ in a way a patch can state.
+
+## Decision
+
+**`createPatch` throws nothing, like `applyMergePatch` and unlike `applyPatch`.** There is nothing to
+report: every pair of JSON values has a patch, so there is no error class and no `reason`. Neither
+input is validated, because neither has a vocabulary to get wrong — a document is whatever it is.
+This puts the library's four entry points at two that can fail (`resolvePointer`, `applyPatch`) and
+two that cannot (`applyMergePatch`, `createPatch`), and in both halves it is the format that decides,
+not a taste for exceptions.
+
+**The patch is made of `add`, `remove` and `replace`; `test`, `move` and `copy` are never produced.**
+`test` states a precondition, which is not part of a difference: a caller who wants an update refused
+when the document has moved under them knows that and prepends their own `test`, and a `test` we
+invented would double the size of every patch for a guarantee nobody asked for. `move` and `copy` say
+nothing a `remove` and an `add` do not already say; producing them means searching the document for a
+value that may be anywhere in it, and the result is a patch that is harder to read and whose indices
+are harder to get right (§ 4.4 counts a `move` inside one array after the removal). The patch
+therefore uses half of the format, deliberately, and stays a patch any RFC 6902 implementation
+applies.
+
+**Differences are described as deeply as the two documents have the same shape.** Two objects are
+compared member by member and two arrays element by element, recursively; anything else — a number
+that became a string, an array that took an object's place — is one `replace` of the value as a whole,
+down to `replace` with `path: ""` for the document itself. This is what makes the patch's size the
+size of the change and not of the document, which is the whole point of the feature. `replace` rather
+than `add` at the root: a document always exists, so replacing it is what is happening, and the two
+behave identically there.
+
+**An array is compared as a sequence, by the fewest element edits.** The elements the two arrays open
+and end with that are equal are trimmed first; what is left is aligned with a dynamic-programming
+edit distance over three edits — an element added, an element removed, two elements read as one that
+changed — and the alignment with the fewest of them is the one described. The alternative, comparing
+element 0 with element 0 and so on, turns one element inserted at the front of a thousand into a
+thousand operations, which is the "whole document again" the request rules out. The trimming is not
+only an optimization of the common case (a long array with one change costs no alignment at all): it
+is what keeps the cost of the alignment, a comparison of every remaining element against every other,
+proportional to the change rather than to the document.
+
+Where two alignments cost the same, an element added or removed is preferred to a pair of elements
+read as one that changed. A pair that ties with an addition or a removal is a pair the alignment has
+no reason to believe in, and reading two unrelated elements as one changed element describes the new
+one member by member instead of sending it once — the common case being a list of records of which
+one was deleted and another edited, where believing the pair would send a whole record that is still
+there.
+
+**Operations on an array are written against the array as the operations before them leave it.** A
+patch is applied in order, so an `add` or a `remove` shifts what follows and the operations after it
+must count in the shifted array: removing the last two of three elements names index 1 twice. The
+alternative — emitting an array's operations back to front, so that every index is the original one —
+costs nothing to implement but gives a patch that reads backwards, and a patch is read by people.
+Indices are written as numbers, never as the `-` of § 4.1, so that an append looks like every other
+addition; `-` would say exactly the same thing.
+
+**The order of the operations is fixed, and tested.** For an object: `from`'s members in its order,
+which is where the removals and the changes are, then the members only `to` has, in its order. For an
+array: the order of the elements. The RFC says nothing about the order of operations that do not
+interfere, so this is not conformance but determinism, as `dl-003` had it for member order: the same
+two documents give the same patch every time, which is what lets the tests pin whole patches and what
+lets a caller cache or compare them.
+
+**A value is never replaced whole because describing its parts turned out to be longer.** When nearly
+every member of an object or element of an array changed, the patch describes each of them, and a
+single `replace` of the parent would have been fewer operations and fewer bytes. The rule was
+considered and rejected: it needs a size threshold, and a threshold makes the patch for a pair of
+documents depend on how big they are — two pairs differing in the same way would get patches of
+different shapes. Every operation in a patch now corresponds to something that really changed, which
+is the property worth keeping; the cost is a patch that can be a few times larger than the document
+in the pathological case where nothing survived. The README states this trade rather than hiding it.
+
+Likewise, the alignment minimizes *element* edits, not operations: an element read as changed is then
+described by its own difference, which may be several operations. Minimizing operations exactly would
+mean diffing every element of one array against every element of the other to learn each pair's true
+cost — the quadratic cost multiplied by the cost of a full comparison — for a patch nobody would
+notice was shorter.
+
+**`src/json-diff.ts` is a module of its own, and `jsonEquals` and `memberOf` move to
+`src/json-value.ts`.** The diff is a second feature *about* RFC 6902, not a second way to apply one,
+and `src/json-patch.ts` is already the longest module in the library; a reader looking for how a patch
+is produced should not have to read how one is applied. It imports the `JsonPatch` and
+`JsonPatchOperation` types from `src/json-patch.ts`, which keeps one declaration of what an operation
+is. `jsonEquals` moves because the diff needs exactly RFC 6902 § 4.6 equality, which `task-002` and
+`dl-003` already recorded as the move a diff feature would make, and `memberOf` because the diff reads
+members the way `applyMergePatch` does, own members only. Both are now where `dl-003` says the
+operations on a JSON value belong.
+
+**`undefined` is read as absent where a document can be without a value, and as `null` where it
+cannot.** `dl-003` reads a patch member holding `undefined` as absent; a diff has a second place to
+worry about, because an operation has to *carry* a value. So: an object member holding `undefined`, in
+either document, is no member — it is not added, not removed and not compared — while an array element
+or a whole document that is `undefined` is read as the `null` that `JSON.stringify` writes for it in an
+array. The point is that `createPatch` always returns a patch `applyPatch` accepts: no operation can
+carry `undefined`, which `applyPatch` rejects as `invalid-patch`, and nothing in RFC 6902 can make a
+document absent (`dl-002`).
+
+**Public surface: `createPatch` alone.** It returns the exported `JsonPatch` type, so a caller can
+hold the result, pass it to `applyPatch` or send it as it is; nothing else is added.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The shape of the patch is now part of the published contract, as the member order of
+  `applyMergePatch` is: changing how arrays are aligned, or the order of an object's operations, would
+  change patches callers compare against. The README and the tests say what the shape is, patch by
+  patch.
+- The patch is round-trippable by construction and is tested as such on every case, but it is *not*
+  the only correct patch, and a caller must not expect another implementation's diff to agree with it.
+- `src/json-value.ts` now holds equality as well as copying, reading and writing. A fifth feature
+  finds `jsonEquals` there; `src/json-patch.ts` and `src/json-merge-patch.ts` lost their private
+  copies and behave as they did.
+- Comparing two arrays whose middles are both long and both entirely different costs a comparison of
+  every element against every other. That is the price of describing insertions in one operation, and
+  the trimming keeps it off the common case — a long document with a small change costs nothing of the
+  sort.
+- Because nothing is thrown, a caller cannot learn from `createPatch` that the two documents were
+  "too different" to describe usefully. If that ever matters, it is a question about the patch it
+  returned — its length — and not an error on this function.
+- A merge-patch counterpart (`createMergePatch`) is now the obvious gap, and would reuse `jsonEquals`
+  and the same member order; it is a different format and would be a different function, not an
+  option on this one.
