+
+# Implement `createPatch`: the difference between two JSON documents as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+## Request
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document instead of the whole
+new version: produce that change as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902). Export `createPatch(from, to)` from
+`src/index.ts`, returning a patch that turns `from` into `to` when applied, mutating neither input,
+and describing what changed and nothing more — sending the whole document again when two versions
+differ in one small place is not acceptable.
+
+## What was done
+
+Test first: `test/json-diff.test.ts` was written against a function that did not exist (red — the
+suite failed to load on the missing export), then `src/json-diff.ts` was added until it passed
+(green), with the suite green from then on.
+
+- `src/json-diff.ts` — new: `createPatch`, the recursive `diff` that describes a difference as deeply
+  as the two values have the same shape, `diffObjects`, and for arrays `diffArrays` with the trimming
+  of the common ends, `editCosts` (the edit-distance table) and `emitEdits` (the walk that writes one
+  operation per edit); plus `asJson`, which is where a non-JSON `undefined` is read.
+- `src/json-value.ts` — `jsonEquals` moved here from `src/json-patch.ts`, and `memberOf` from
+  `src/json-merge-patch.ts`, both unchanged: the diff needs RFC 6902 § 4.6 equality and the
+  own-member read, and `dl-003` had already recorded that a diff feature is what would move
+  `jsonEquals`.
+- `src/json-patch.ts` and `src/json-merge-patch.ts` — import those two instead of declaring them; no
+  behaviour change.
+- `src/index.ts` — adds `createPatch` to the public surface, next to the other RFC 6902 exports. No
+  new type and no error class: a patch is the already exported `JsonPatch`, and comparing cannot fail.
+- `test/json-diff.test.ts` — 39 tests: equal documents, the document as a whole, objects, arrays
+  (append, truncate, insertion and removal at the front and in the middle, an element changed, an
+  element changed inside, reordering, an insertion and a removal in one array, nesting), the "nothing
+  more than what changed" cases on a 1000-element and a deeply nested document, the patch as a value
+  of its own, `__proto__` and inherited properties, and the non-JSON `undefined`.
+- `README.md` — a feature section with the usage, a table of how each kind of difference is described,
+  and the points worth knowing: the three operations never produced, the array alignment and the
+  index shifting, the fixed order, the deliberate trade on documents of which nothing survived, the
+  new-value promise, and that nothing is thrown.
+
+Behaviour:
+
+- the patch is made of `add`, `remove` and `replace`; `test`, `move` and `copy` are never produced;
+- two objects are compared member by member, two arrays element by element, recursively, so only what
+  differs is mentioned, at any depth; anything else is one `replace` of the whole value, `path: ""`
+  for the document itself;
+- an array is compared as a sequence, by the fewest element edits, after trimming the equal elements
+  at both ends: one element inserted at the front of a thousand is one `add`, and the elements around
+  a change are not sent;
+- an array's operations are written against the array as the operations before them leave it, as
+  RFC 6902 applies them: removing the last two of three elements names index 1 twice;
+- the order is fixed: for an object, `from`'s members in its order (the removals and the changes),
+  then the members only `to` has, in its order;
+- member names are escaped into their pointers (`a/b` → `/a~1b`), and a member named `__proto__` is
+  the plain member it is, read with `Object.hasOwn` and written as an ordinary member by `applyPatch`;
+  an inherited property is not a member either document has;
+- the patch shares no object or array with either document, the values it carries being copies taken
+  from `to`, and neither document is changed;
+- comparing cannot fail, so nothing is thrown;
+- `undefined`, which is no JSON value, is read as absent for an object member in either document, and
+  as `null` for an array element or a document as a whole — the two places a patch would have to carry
+  it, which it cannot.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 174 tests, 174 pass, 0 fail (39 new for the diff; the 135 for JSON Pointer, JSON Patch
+  and JSON Merge Patch unchanged and still passing after `jsonEquals` and `memberOf` moved into
+  `src/json-value.ts`).
+- `npm run typecheck` — clean, sources and tests, `strict` on.
+- `npm run build` — clean; `dist/index.js` was imported and exercised with the README's example and
+  with the doc comment's, both printing what they claim, and with `applyMergePatch` and
+  `resolvePointer` to confirm the moved helpers did not disturb them.
+- Every test asserts the patch itself *and* that applying it to `from` yields `to` *and* that neither
+  document was changed, through one helper: a patch that rewrote the whole document would pass a round
+  trip, so the operations are pinned case by case.
+- Round trip under fuzzing, as a one-off check outside the suite (the suite stays deterministic, as
+  the determinism directive asks): 30 000 pairs of generated documents — nested objects and arrays,
+  member names needing escaping, `__proto__`, repeated and equal pairs — all satisfied
+  `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to)) == to`, left both documents unchanged, and gave the same
+  patch when asked twice.
+- Cost on large documents, measured the same way: 5000 elements with one changed, or one inserted,
+  takes a few milliseconds and gives one operation; 200 levels of nesting with one changed leaf gives
+  one operation of 848 bytes.
+- Red was observed before green: the first run of the new suite failed on the missing export, and no
+  test was written to fail artificially.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- `dl-004-shape-and-semantics-of-createpatch-what-a-diff-describes-how-arrays-are-aligned-which-operations-it-never-produces`
+  — nothing thrown and nothing validated; the three operations never produced; describing a difference
+  as deeply as the shapes agree; the array alignment, its tie-break and the trimming of the common
+  ends; operations written against the array as patched so far, with numeric indices; the fixed order
+  as determinism; the refusal of a size threshold that would replace a value whole; the new
+  `src/json-diff.ts` module and the move of `jsonEquals` and `memberOf`; and `undefined` read as
+  absent or as `null` depending on whether a document can be without a value there.
+
+## Follow-up for the next features
+
+- A merge-patch counterpart, `createMergePatch(from, to)`, is the obvious gap: the same comparison
+  written in the other format, reusing `jsonEquals`, and carrying that format's limits (it cannot
+  express a member set to `null`, which is why it cannot be derived from this patch).
+- `src/json-value.ts` now holds equality too. A fifth feature uses `jsonEquals` from there rather than
+  comparing with `JSON.stringify`, which would depend on member order.
+- If a caller ever needs the patch to be minimal in operations rather than in element edits, that is a
+  different cost model in `editCosts` — the alignment is the one place it is decided.
